The baseline is already minimal in arithmetic: one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add. The only remaining lever is the add of B.

Adding the plaintext B costs about 2.5 ms for encoding. The alternative is to encrypt-free fold B in some other way, but that needs a ciphertext operand, and the only ciphertext is x.

One option is to compute (x + B/A) * A. That needs a plaintext divide, which is not allowed, and it would still need an add of a plaintext vector, so it saves nothing.

Another option is to change the order: the add happens after the rescale, at the deeper level, where it is cheaper. This is probably what already happens. If CHEHAB instead adds B before the rescale, the plaintext would have to be encoded at the doubled scale. Either way the cost is the same.

I can't see a circuit with fewer operations, so I'll try a structurally different form, `(x*a) + b` with explicit ordering. CHEHAB will probably normalize it away, but it is the only variation available.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext y = t + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: avoid the plaintext-vector add (encoding, ~2.5 ms) and the separate multiply. Without changing the function, I can't remove B, since only x is a ciphertext. But I can try to make the add happen before the multiply, so that it is applied at the first level, where the encoding is the same cost, and see whether CHEHAB produces a different circuit: y = (x + B/A) * A. This needs B/A, which is not available as an input (no plaintext arithmetic).

An alternative is to use a ciphertext-ciphertext trick, but it costs more (30 ms).

The only legal structural change is to put the add into the multiplication stage. Here I compute x*a and add b through a different route: (x*a - (-b)). The negation of a plaintext is plaintext arithmetic, which is not allowed. So I'll keep the subtraction form on the ciphertext side: y = -((-(x*a)) - b). This uses negations, which are cheap and may change the circuit's operation counts. It is unlikely to be faster, but it is a valid variation.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext u = -t;
  Ciphertext v = u - b;
  Ciphertext y = -v;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: the baseline circuit is x*A (plaintext multiply plus rescale) followed by a plaintext add of B. The remaining cost is mostly the plaintext encodings and the rescale. I can't drop B, and x is the only ciphertext, so no cheaper arithmetic form exists. What I can change is where the add sits relative to the rescale.

A form that might change the circuit: the add of B before the rescale. That would need B at the doubled scale, which CHEHAB handles on its own, so I can't force it from the DSL.

Another form is to multiply by A with a different structure: x*A = (x + x)*(A/2) needs a plaintext halving, which is not allowed. Likewise, splitting into two multiplies would only add cost.

So I resubmit the minimal form, using `+=` on the product. CHEHAB probably normalizes this to the baseline circuit, but no cheaper legal circuit seems to exist.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x;
  y *= a;
  y += b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: the baseline already uses the minimum arithmetic (one plaintext multiply, one rescale, one plaintext add). The only remaining way to change the circuit is to try a different split of the work, so I'll test whether any legal form is cheaper.

One candidate: y = (x + B*A⁻¹) * A is impossible, since plaintext-plaintext arithmetic is not allowed. So I'll use the other available form, a ciphertext-side restructuring that gives the add a different shape: y = x*A + B, written as x*A - (0 - B) is not legal either.

The one distinct legal variant is to add B to x at the input level with a separate plaintext multiply, and so on. Each such variant only adds operations. Since an attempt must differ from the earlier circuits, I'm submitting a variant that is mathematically equal but built differently: x*A + B with the product split in two halves, (x*A) computed once and added to B through a subtraction of the negated ciphertext. This is expected to be about as fast as the baseline, and may be slightly slower.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext n = -t;
  Ciphertext y = -(n - b);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline is already at the arithmetic minimum: one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add. I can't fold B into the multiply, because that would need B/A, which is plaintext arithmetic. This attempt keeps the same operations but writes the final step as a plaintext-minus-ciphertext subtraction followed by a negation, giving a slightly different structure. It is unlikely to beat the baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext y = -(b - t);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 5 was my own algebra mistake: -(b - t) equals t - b, not t + b. The corrected form is b - (-t) = b + t.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext n = -t;
  Ciphertext y = b - n;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't see a cheaper circuit. The baseline already has one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add. Removing any of them would need plaintext-plaintext arithmetic such as B/A, which CKKS rules forbid, or extra ciphertext operations. I'm resubmitting the minimal form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I haven't found a legal circuit with fewer operations than the baseline. It needs one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add. Every alternative I tried either collapsed to the same circuit or added negations, which was slower. For the last attempt I'm keeping the baseline form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```
